A service (a daemon) is a program that runs in the background without direct user interaction.

Service       	Purpose
sshd     	Allows remote SSH connections
nginx	    Hosts websites
docker  	Manages containers
cups	    Handles printing
NetworkManager	Manages network connections
cron	     Runs scheduled tasks

System service management is the process of starting, stopping, monitoring, configuring, and automatically running background
programs (services) that the operating system or applications depend on.
- On most modern Linux distributions, this is handled by systemd, using the systemctl command.
- service - Legacy command that works with both SysV init and systemd (acts as a wrapper)
- Both systemctl and service are used to manage system services in Linux, but they operate at different levels.

SYSTEMCTL
Syntax: systemctl [OPTIONS] COMMAND [UNIT_NAME]
Core Commands for Service Management
--start & stop #different from enable, starts/stops now ONLY
 sudo systemctl start nginx #Start a Service immediately, will be unavailable after reboot # Use sudo for system services
 sudo systemctl stop nginx  #Stops the service immediately, Does NOT persist across reboots
--restart & reload
 sudo systemctl restart nginx     # Stops and starts the service again.
 sudo systemctl reload nginx      # Reloads the service's configuration without fully restarting it. #NOTE. not all service reloads, if it doesn't, use restart
 sudo systemctl reload-or-restart nginx  # Reload if possible, else restart
           #Restart: Complete stop and start (disruptive)
           #Reload: Graceful re-read of config files (non-disruptive if supported)
--status
 systemctl status nginx  #Status Check, Shows: Active state (active/inactive), PID ,Memory usage, Recent log entries, Load state (loaded/not-loaded)
--Exit Codes for scripting
 systemctl is-active nginx    # Returns 0 if active
 systemctl is-enabled nginx   # Returns 0 if enabled
 systemctl is-failed nginx    # Returns 0 if failed
-- Enable/Disable # enable or disable persists reboots 
 sudo systemctl enable nginx    # enables nginx for future boots, will still be disabled until reboot
 sudo systemctl disable nginx   # Disable at boot
 sudo systemctl enable --now nginx    # Enable & starts immediately, even after reboot
 sudo systemctl disable --now nginx   # Disables &  stop immediately, even after reboot
-- Mask/Unmask (Prevent Starting)
 sudo systemctl mask nginx     # stop & lock the service from boot, also blocks from being started by other dependencies, Prevents manual start too.
 sudo systemctl unmask nginx   # Unlock service, its other app or process dependencies can use it.

