journalctl - The Systemd Journal Viewer
Overview: journalctl is the command-line utility for querying and viewing logs collected by systemd's journal,
a centralized logging system that replaces traditional log files in /var/log/. It collects logs from:
- System kernel messages
- System services (via systemd)
- User applications (if configured)
- Boot processes

Basic Syntax
journalctl [OPTIONS] [MATCHES]
Core Commands & Common Use Cases
-- View All Logs
journalctl                    # All logs (most recent first)
journalctl --reverse          # Oldest first (chronological)

-- Filter by Service/Unit
journalctl -u nginx           # Logs for nginx service
journalctl -u sshd            # Logs for SSH daemon
journalctl -u docker          # Logs for Docker
journalctl -u nginx -u mysql  # Multiple services combined

-- Follow Live Logs (tail -f equivalent)
journalctl -f                 # Follow all logs
journalctl -u nginx -f        # Follow nginx logs only

-- Filter by Time
journalctl --since "2026-07-20 10:00:00"           # Since specific date/time
journalctl --until "2026-07-20 12:00:00"           # Until specific date/time
journalctl --since "1 hour ago"                    # Relative time
journalctl --since "2026-07-20" --until "2026-07-21"
journalctl --since "10 minutes ago" -u nginx

        Time formats accepted:
  - "2026-07-20 10:30:45" - Full date/time
  - "2026-07-20" - Entire day
  - "10:30:00" - Today at that time
  - "1 hour ago" - Human-friendly
  - "yesterday", "today", "tomorrow"

-- Filter by Boot
journalctl -b                  # Current boot logs
journalctl -b -1               # Previous boot logs
journalctl -b -2               # Boot before that
journalctl --list-boots        # List all boots with IDs
journalctl -b 7a8b9c...        # Specific boot ID

--Filter by Priority/Level
journalctl -p err              # Errors and above (emerg, alert, crit, err)
journalctl -p warning          # Warning and above
journalctl -p 3                # Numeric priority (0=emerg, 7=debug)

Priority Levels:
 Code   Name       Description 
 0     emerg     System is unusable 
 1     alert     Action must be taken immediately 
 2     crit      Critical conditions 
 3     err       Error conditions 
 4    warning    Warning conditions 
 5    notice    Normal but significant 
 6    info      Informational 
 7   debug      Debug-level messages 

--Filter by Facility
journalctl -f SYSLOG_FACILITY=0    # Kernel messages
journalctl -f SYSLOG_FACILITY=1    # User-level messages
journalctl -f SYSLOG_FACILITY=4    # System daemons

--Filter by Process/Command
journalctl _PID=1234               # Specific PID
journalctl _COMM=nginx             # Process name
journalctl _UID=1000               # User ID
journalctl _GID=1000               # Group ID
journalctl _EXE=/usr/bin/nginx     # Executable path

--Filter by Message Content
journalctl -g "error"              # Grep for "error" (case-insensitive)
journalctl -g "ERROR.*failed"      # Regex pattern
journalctl -g "502 Bad Gateway" -u nginx

